    <instruction>35, 36, and 37 The Riemann zeta-function The function $$\zeta$$ defined by $$\zeta(s) = \sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{s}}$$ where $$s$$ is a complex number, is called the Riemann zeta function.</instruction>
    <exercise number="35" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For which real numbers $$x$$ is $$\zeta(x)$$ defined?</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\left( {1,\infty} \right)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="36" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Leonhard Euler was able to calculate the exact sum of the $$p$$ -series with $$p = 2$$ :</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\zeta(2) = \sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{2}} = \frac{\pi^{2}}{6}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Use this fact to find the sum of each series.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 2}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{2}}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 3}^{\infty}\frac{1}{{(n + 1)}^{2}}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{{(2n)}^{2}}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="37" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Euler also found the sum of the $$p$$ -series with $$p = 4$$ :</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\zeta(4) = \sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^{4}} = \frac{\pi^{4}}{90}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>Use Euler’s result to find the sum of the series.</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{n = 1}^{\infty}\left( \frac{3}{n} \right)^{4}$$</item>
          <item>$$\sum\limits_{k = 5}^{\infty}\frac{1}{{(k - 2)}^{4}}$$</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$\frac{9}{10}\pi^{4}$$</paragraph>
        <paragraph>$$\frac{1}{90}\pi^{4} - \frac{17}{16}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="38" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Find the partial sum $$s_{10}$$ of the series $$\sum_{n = 1}^{\infty}{1 \slash n^{4}}$$ . Estimate the error in using $$s_{10}$$ as an approximation to the sum of the series.</item>
          <item>Use (3) with $$n = 10$$ to give an improved estimate of the sum.</item>
          <item>Compare your estimate in part (b) with the exact value given in Exercise 37.</item>
          <item>Find a value of $$n$$ so that $$s_{n}$$ is within $$0.00001$$ of the sum.</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="39" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
